From 0cf7b2814ed423dd7f74f5e8ab9ea1adddcd8288 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tommaso Sacchetti <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:36:13 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: msm8996: mark the CPU power collapse state
 local-timer-stop

The per-CPU architected timer stops while a core is in the PSCI
standalone power collapse state, but the idle state is not flagged
local-timer-stop, so cpuidle does not hand timer duty to the broadcast
timer and the core can sleep through its own next event.

On a Xiaomi Mi 5, enabling PSCI cpuidle without this hung the boot at
about 0.86 s, right after the RPM regulators come up. With the flag, all
four cores spend about 98% of idle time in the state and survive an idle
plus 4-core load soak.

Signed-off-by: Tommaso Sacchetti
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8996.dtsi | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8996.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8996.dtsi
index abdaa41..3c0046f 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8996.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8996.dtsi
@@ -139,6 +139,8 @@
 				compatible = "arm,idle-state";
 				idle-state-name = "standalone-power-collapse";
 				arm,psci-suspend-param = <0x00000004>;
+				/* the per-CPU arch timer stops in power collapse */
+				local-timer-stop;
 				entry-latency-us = <130>;
 				exit-latency-us = <80>;
 				min-residency-us = <300>;
